#data

/ A numeric variable stores raw bytes in the data section.
/ @name  0xVALUE   -> defines a symbol "name" with VALUE as its data.
/ The VALUE hex digits determine the byte count: 0xFFFF = 2 bytes (FF FF).
@variable 0xFFFF

/ A string variable stores null-terminated text.
/ 'name "text"   -> defines a symbol "name" with "text\0" as its data.
'string "string"

#text

/ Function declaration: [name] {core position} (arguments)
/   core     - which core this handler runs on (0-3, or * for all)
/   position - which interrupt vector slot (0-64) or empty for start function
/   ()       - named arguments (future use, parsed but not yet resolved)
/ A function with no position (like {0}) is a start function (interrupt > 64).
/ The compiler writes its address into the instruction vector at 0x000000.

[start] {0} ()
/ LDI - Load Immediate (4 bytes: 32 0R NN NN)
/ Loads a 16-bit value into a register.
/   $0   = register 0 (low nibble of the "0R" byte)
/   @variable = symbol lookup, expands to 0xFF 0xFF
@LDI $0 @variable

/ @& gives the address of a variable instead of its inline value,
/ useful for loading from data memory with RLD/RLM (see README).
/ RLD - Relative Load (4 bytes: 39 SR MM MM)
/   Example: @RLD $1 @&variable
/   $1         = destination register
/   @&variable = address of the variable in memory

/ HLT - Halt (1 byte: 00)
@HLT
[/start]

/ Mutex Permission Failure interrupt handler.
/ The asterisk (*) means this handler applies to ALL cores.
/ The position 2 means it hooks interrupt vector 2.
/ When a mutex permission failure occurs on any core,
/ the CPU will dispatch to this handler.
[mutex_permission_failure] [* 2] ()
@HLT
[/mutex_permission_failure]
